  <section class="wrap fiche-body">
    <div>
      <h2>The vibe</h2>
      <p>Part of the Hippodrome beach, in Cagnes-sur-Mer, is reserved for dogs: the town presents it as their swimming area, between groyne 1 and groyne 2, at the western end of Cagnes' coastline, facing the racecourse and at the boundary with Villeneuve-Loubet. It is the only beach in the commune where dogs are admitted: the Cagnes tourist office describes it as the only "dogs admitted" beach of the town's 3.5 km of pebble beaches.</p>

      <h2>What the rules say</h2>
      <p>Municipal order no. 2024/0805 on beach hygiene (article 11) forbids, from 1 June to 30 September, swimming and beach access for domestic animals, with the exception of a limited, signposted area at the western end of the coastline, facing the racecourse, between groyne no. 1 and groyne no. 2. Guide and assistance dogs, as well as rescue dogs on an official mission, may access the beaches. During the summer season, this stretch alone is therefore open to your dog; for the rest of the year, the order read sets no other rule.</p>
      <p>The area also appears on the town's "Cagnes-sur-Mer, amie des animaux" page, which lists it with the dog parks (play areas) set up in several districts: square Gendarme Devron, parc de la Méditerranée on the promenade de la Plage, parc des Canebiers, prairie Marcel Pagnol, among others, all accessible 24/7 and most equipped with drinking fountains and shaded areas.</p>

      <h2>Leash, supervision and seabed</h2>
      <p>On the leash, the official sources do not say the same thing. The Nice Côte d'Azur metropolitan tourist office (listing updated on 05/03/2026) specifies that dogs may be off-leash but must not be left unsupervised or disturb the tranquillity of other users. The town, for its part, reminds people, for life in town, to keep their dog on a leash and pick up waste; waste bags are handed out at the main town hall and the branch town halls. The beach is pebbles, like the rest of the Cagnes coastline: check that your dog's paw pads cope with hot pebbles in summer.</p>
      <p>The sources read say nothing about a shower or water point in this area: bring fresh water and a bowl.</p>
      <div class="callout">Coco's tip: this is a good stop between Nice and Villeneuve-Loubet if you're combining several dog-friendly spots in one day.</div>

      <h2>Nearby</h2>
      <p>The area is at the boundary with Villeneuve-Loubet, whose Vaugrenier beach is next door. The Cagnes dog parks (parc de la Méditerranée, parc des Canebiers) let you stretch your dog's legs in town before or after the beach.</p>
    </div>

